        <paragraph>$$x_{1},y,y_{1},$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$y=-2.333x+6.667. y=-2.333x+6.667.$$ Answers may vary. $$y min =-10, y max =10 y min =-10, y max =10$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <heading level="1">Extensions</heading>
    <exercise number="50" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Starting with the point-slope formula $$m$$ solve this expression for $$Ax + By = C$$ in terms of $$y$$ and $$A,B,C$$ .</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="51" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Starting with the standard form of an equation $$x$$ solve this expression for $$7x - 5y = 25.$$ in terms of $$(-1,1),(2,0),(3,3)$$ and $$(0,4)$$ . Then put the expression in slope-intercept form.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$y=- A B x+ C B y=- A B x+ C B$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="52" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Use the above derived formula to put the following standard equation in slope intercept form: $$\frac{1}{12}.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="53" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Given that the following coordinates are the vertices of a rectangle, prove that this truly is a rectangle by showing the slopes of the sides that meet are perpendicular.</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$x$$ and $$y$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$The slope for (-1,1)to (0,4)is 3. The slope for (-1,1)to (2,0)is -1 3 . The slope for (2,0)to (3,3)is 3. The slope for (0,4)to (3,3)is -1 3 . The slope for (-1,1)to (0,4)is 3. The slope for (-1,1)to (2,0)is -1 3 . The slope for (2,0)to (3,3)is 3. The slope for (0,4)to (3,3)is -1 3 .$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Yes they are perpendicular.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="54" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the slopes of the diagonals in the previous exercise. Are they perpendicular?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <heading level="1">Real-World Applications</heading>
    <exercise number="55" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The slope for a wheelchair ramp for a home has to be $$p = 3x + 4y,$$ If the vertical distance from the ground to the door bottom is 2.5 ft, find the distance the ramp has to extend from the home in order to comply with the needed slope.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>30 ft</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="56" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>If the profit equation for a small business selling $$y$$ number of item one and $$p = \$453 \text{and} x = 75.$$ number of item two is $$y = 45 + .25x,$$ find the $$x$$ value when $$\$63.75,$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>For the following exercises, use this scenario: The cost of renting a car is \$45/wk plus \$0.25/mi traveled during that week. An equation to represent the cost would be $$y=45+.25x, y=45+.25x,$$ where $$x x$$ is the number of miles traveled.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="57" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>What is your cost if you travel 50 mi?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>\$57.50</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="58" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>If your cost were $$\$63.75, \$63.75,$$ how many miles were you charged for traveling?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="59" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Suppose you have a maximum of \$100 to spend for the car rental. What would be the maximum number of miles you could travel?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>220 mi</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
